% ============================================================================
% mammal-origin — for each Australian mammal in animal-classes.adj, whether
% it is introduced (non-native) or marsupial, as the SAME Australian Museum
% spans also state (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `animal-classes.adj`
% (`animal_class(animal, class)`, WHICH vertebrate class an animal belongs
% to — cat, kangaroo, fox, rabbit, bandicoot, quoll, koala are all just
% `mammal`). That table's own header already quotes, verbatim, two
% Australian Museum spans that split those same seven mammals into two
% disjoint origin groups — a distinction the one-class-per-animal schema had
% no room for:
%
%   "introduced mammals such as cats, foxes and rabbits"
%     → cat, fox, rabbit are INTRODUCED (non-native) mammals.
%
%   "marsupials like kangaroos, bandicoots, quolls and the Koala"
%     → kangaroo, bandicoot, quoll, koala are MARSUPIAL mammals.
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? mammal_origin(kangaroo, $Origin)   % marsupial
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, an ordinary two-column EXACT
% lookup): each `row` lowers to a ground relation `mammal_origin(animal,
% origin)` carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD
% binding query — the engine returns the origin WITH its source, on the CPU,
% with zero model calls. All seven mammals `animal-classes.adj` ships have an
% origin stated in this SAME cited span, so this sibling covers the full
% seven-mammal domain with no abstention. The query also runs BACKWARD —
% bind an origin and recall which mammals share it:
%
%     ? mammal_origin($Animal, introduced)
%       % cat ; fox ; rabbit
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME two Australian Museum
% spans already quoted inside `animal-classes.adj`'s own header — no new
% WebFetch, only a different schema decoding the origin half of each
% already-verified quote. `trust authoritative` — the same tier
% `animal-classes.adj` already carries (Australian Museum, a natural-history
% museum and primary reference).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table mammal_origin {
    columns animal, origin

    row (cat, introduced)        % Australian Museum: "introduced mammals such as cats, foxes and rabbits"
    row (fox, introduced)        % Australian Museum: "introduced mammals such as cats, foxes and rabbits"
    row (rabbit, introduced)     % Australian Museum: "introduced mammals such as cats, foxes and rabbits"
    row (kangaroo, marsupial)    % Australian Museum: "marsupials like kangaroos, bandicoots, quolls and the Koala"
    row (bandicoot, marsupial)   % Australian Museum: "marsupials like kangaroos, bandicoots, quolls and the Koala"
    row (quoll, marsupial)       % Australian Museum: "marsupials like kangaroos, bandicoots, quolls and the Koala"
    row (koala, marsupial)       % Australian Museum: "marsupials like kangaroos, bandicoots, quolls and the Koala"

    source "introduced mammals such as cats, foxes and rabbits"
    locator "https://australian.museum/learn/animals/mammals/"
    trust authoritative

    cites "marsupials like kangaroos, bandicoots, quolls and the Koala" locator "https://australian.museum/learn/animals/mammals/"
}
